#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"
Go through the heb/, eng/ and the current working directory's many
[0123456789]-* subdirs where the final tex files live, and make sure
that the merged names like "Samuel" and "Kings" are in the table of
contents, but that the original "I Samuel" and "II Kings" and so on
names are in the verse by verse headings and the page headings, and
to do that you'll first have to check the heb/ files and use them
to make everything consistent. I want *11* books in the table of
contents in the end, with the ambiguous ones being always called
*Samuel* and *Kings* and *Ezra* in the table of contents, but
the chapters inside those books and the naming of the files that
make them up should reflect the "I Samuel" and "II Samuel" and
"I Kings" and "II Kings" and "Ezra" and "Nehemiah" naming of the
books and numbering of the chapters within them. Keep checking
until you're confident you got it right. This shouldn't require
much or any changing the heb/ or eng/ files and probably not
any changing of the combined tex files, but you will have to
change a good number of filenames and you'll have to look inside
a good deal of files to make sure we know where things should
start and end. And you'll have to change master.tex to make
sure the table of contents reflects the 11 books but the
chapter by chapter includes reflect the filenames that you
decide are best to maintain this sort of system, which is
likely going to be something like 08-samuel/1-02.tex for
1st samuel chapter 2, with the 08 being the position of the
book within the other books of the overall volume. Go!
EOF
